Omarchy: a keyboard-driven desktop and terminal on Debian 13 (arm64),
running on unkernel. The packages are Debian's own.

Desktop

    omarchy desktop         start sway on the display (the page's GPU option,
                            or the CLI's --gpu). Super+Shift+E leaves it.
    Super+Alt+Space         the menu: install, update, lock, power.
    Super+Space             the launcher (fuzzel).
    Super+Return            a terminal (foot).
    omarchy keys            every keybinding.

Terminal

    bash with starship (prompt), zoxide (z DIR), fzf (Ctrl-R history,
    Ctrl-T files, Alt-C directories), ripgrep (rg), fd (fdfind), bat (cat),
    eza (ls, ll, la), btop (top), lazygit (lg), neovim (v), tmux (prefix
    Ctrl-a), git, jq, curl.

Packages

    omarchy install PKG...  or sudo apt-get install PKG...
    omarchy update          apt update and upgrade.

    The root file system is read-only. Packages and changes go to the
    overlay: they stay across reboots when the page keeps a data disk, and
    are lost otherwise.

Lock screen

    The lock needs a password. Set one first:

        sudo passwd user

    Then Super+Escape locks the screen. Without a password, omarchy lock
    refuses, so the screen cannot lock itself shut.

Configuration

    ~/.config: sway, foot, fuzzel, mako, starship.toml, nvim, btop, tmux,
    lazygit, bat. Everything is in Tokyo Night.

Limits

    No sound, no Nerd Font, no network tools beyond curl and git. The
    desktop is for one user, on the display the page or the CLI draws.
